 "result": "Three failing tests don't come from the `partitioned` cookie TypeError, which is the main root cause behind the other 21.\n\n1. **`tests/test_request.py::test_limit_config`**\n   - It fails at `assert r.max_form_memory_size == 500_000`, getting `None`.\n   - With no app context, `src/tessel/wrappers.py:110-111` falls back to `super().max_form_memory_size`. The installed Werkzeug 3.0.3 doesn't supply the 3.1 default of `500_000`.\n   - It is a different API gap from the cookie one, but it has the same underlying trigger: the installed Werkzeug is older than 3.1.\n\n2. **`tests/test_helpers.py::TestStreaming::test_async_view`**\n   - It fails with `RuntimeError: Install Tessel with the 'async' extra in order to use async views.`, raised at `src/tessel/app.py:1096` in `ensure_sync`.\n   - The optional async dependency (asgiref) isn't installed. This has nothing to do with the Werkzeug version.\n\n3. **`tests/test_helpers.py::test_open_resource_exceptions[a]`**\n   - I could not confirm the cause. It passes when run alone, and the `w`, `x` and `r+` variants pass in the full run.\n   - It most likely fails because of an unraisable-exception warning from the generator leaked by `test_stream_keeps_session`. That would make it a side effect of the cookie failure, but this is inference, not something I verified.\n\nThe teardown `ERROR` on `test_stream_keeps_session` isn't a separate test. It is a consequence of that test's own cookie failure.",
